ew cooperation (know-it-all, hot temper) - negative, professional communication (hot temper) - negative, responsibility (takes responsibility seriously) - positive, adaptability unknown.\n\nA: reliability unknown, stress resilience unknown, technical (spatial vision, complex connections), decision quality unknown, attention unknown, crew cooperation (show-off) - negative, professional communication (not open to new ideas) - negative, responsibility (leadership) maybe positive, adaptability (not open) - negative.\n\nThus C seems strongest overall across core safety criteria, despite communication issues. B has strong stress and technical but communication issues also. Which is more critical? For long-distance, stress resilience is crucial, but C also keeps calm in crisis. So C also has stress resilience. So C may be overall best.\n\nThus I might argue that despite communication concerns, C's reliability and decision quality outweigh B's communication issues, and C also shows calm under pressure. So I could shift recommendation back to C, or support B. Need to decide.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't discussed D much, but D has many cooperation issues. A also has issues.\n\nThus likely best is C. I can argue that C's reliability and quick correct decisions, plus calmness, are top criteria, and his communication difficulty can be mitigated with training, whereas B's nasty remarks are more severe for crew cooperation. So I will recommend Candidate C.\n\nThus my vote: Candidate C.\n\nNow produce public message: reference Anna's recommendation, Sofia's shift to B, and explain why I think C remains best.\n\nInclude explicit facts: 100% reliable, keeps calm in crisis, makes correct decisions quickly, understands complicated technology, creates positive atmosphere with crew (if we can use that). Also note communication difficulty and egocentric, but argue that crew atmosphere positive suggests cooperation is okay, and adaptability concerns less critical than reliability.\n\nThus pr